WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:01.709
This universe is finite.

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:03.599
Its resource is finite.

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.129
If life is left unchecked.

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:07.319
Life will cease to exist.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.209
That's Thanos, the Marvel super

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:10.529
villain who kills half of all

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:12.419
humanity with the snap of

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.699
his fingers in Avengers Infinity

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.799
War. As Marion Tupy and Gail Pooley

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:17.849
argue in their new book,

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.199
Superabundance.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.239
Thanos is channeling millennia

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:22.919
old critiques of progress and

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:24.509
population growth.

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:26.639
He's also flat out wrong.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.229
The past 200 years have seen

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.089
historically huge increases in

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:31.319
the number of people living on

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:32.309
planet Earth.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.079
In 1800, there were 1 billion

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:34.859
people.

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:36.899
Now there are 8 billion.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:38.819
We are flourishing more than ever

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.739
before and living longer,

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.689
more productive lives.

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:44.369
Tupy and Pooley chart how the real

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.709
prices of our most basic necessities

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:48.569
and virtually all luxuries

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:50.189
have declined over time.

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:51.779
And how free markets and human

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:53.669
innovation mean that our planet

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:55.590
is infinitely bountiful.

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:02.449
Gail Pooley, Marion Tupy.

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:03.450
Thanks for talkin to Reason.

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:04.848
My pleasure.

00:01:04.849 --> 00:01:06.079
Glad to be here.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:07.939
All right. So let's start

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:10.249
with the elevator pitch for

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:12.109
superabundance,

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:13.759
the story of population growth,

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:15.469
innovation and human flourishing on

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:17.329
an infinitely bountiful

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:18.619
planet.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:19.609
Marian, why don't you start?

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.619
What's what's the elevator pitch for

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:22.459
this book?

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:24.619
Well, people have been wondering

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:26.779
about the relationship between

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:28.819
resource abundance and population

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:30.199
growth for at least two and a half

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:31.579
thousand years. The ancient

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:32.719
Egyptians saw it.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:34.669
The ancient Greeks thought about it,

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:36.019
the ancient Romans thought about it,

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:37.189
the Chinese thought about it, the

00:01:37.190 --> 00:01:38.689
Indians thought about it.

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:40.669
And really,

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:42.529
people have always had this sort of

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:44.479
ambiguous feeling about population

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:46.699
growth. But, 200 years ago

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:48.559
and really over the last 200 years,

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.359
specifically since Thomas Malthus

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:52.189
published his famous essay on the

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:54.379
principle of population, most

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:56.209
people have been generally

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:58.279
negative to to our population

00:01:58.280 --> 00:01:59.269
growth. In other words, there was an

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:01.699
expectation that as population grew,

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:03.589
resources would become more

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:05.509
expensive, therefore scarcer, and

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:07.459
there would be some kind of calamity

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:10.189
maybe running out of food, the

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:12.049
starvation, so the world would

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:13.909
be would be destroyed as we

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:15.859
know it. Now, in the last 200

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:17.743
years, specifically between 1820

00:02:17.744 --> 00:02:18.744
and 2022,

00:02:20.270 --> 00:02:21.529
population of the world has

00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:23.509
increased from 1 billion people

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:25.879
to 8 billion people.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:28.369
And yet what we found

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:30.199
in our book, by looking at hundreds

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:33.319
of different commodities fuels,

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:35.329
minerals, metals, even

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:36.859
finished goods and some services,

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:38.809
everything has become cheaper

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:39.949
in terms of time price.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:41.839
People simply have to work less

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:43.789
and less time in order to

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:45.709
buy things which

00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:47.299
which are essential goods and

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:49.069
commodities. In order to survive.

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:50.389
Things are just getting cheaper.

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:52.699
And that tells us that

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:54.859
every human being on average creates

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:57.559
more value than he or she consumes.

00:02:57.560 --> 00:02:59.809
That human beings are inventors

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:01.639
and innovators, and they

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:03.589
can create more value

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:05.419
forever, thereby increasing

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:06.169
standards of living.

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:08.149
And that is I mean, we'll get into

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:09.439
this, but you guys spend a lot of

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:11.179
time documenting the great

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:13.489
enrichment, which is the term that

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:15.349
Deirdre McCloskey and other economic

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:17.209
historians have used

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:19.189
for the past 200 years,

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:20.149
roughly the period of

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:22.039
industrialization where,

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:23.839
you know, things went from being

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:25.999
pretty flat for a thousand years

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:27.859
or more to getting better

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:28.699
and better.

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:30.529
Gail, one of the kind

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:31.530
of

00:03:32.450 --> 00:03:34.609
figure the you know, the spirit

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:36.679
animal of your book is Julian

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:38.599
Simon. Can you explain who

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:40.519
he is and why he's so

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.559
important to the to the project

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:43.459
here?

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:45.679
Well, Julian Simon actually

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:47.899
was this obscure kind of economist

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:50.239
and was teaching at university.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.129
And there was a book

00:03:52.130 --> 00:03:54.469
that was published in 1968 by

00:03:54.470 --> 00:03:56.599
Stanford biologist Paul

00:03:56.600 --> 00:03:58.699
Ehrlich, and it the title

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:00.649
was "The Population

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:02.179
Bomb." And he makes these claims

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:04.039
about, "look, we're facing this

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:05.239
extinction because there are too

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:07.519
many people." And Julian actually

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:09.649
said that he when he originally

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:10.819
read the book, he thought, well,

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:12.739
this this theory seems

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:13.849
to be reasonable.

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:16.278
But as he began to kind of check

00:04:16.279 --> 00:04:19.249
the facts, what he discovered,

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:21.139
he said, to his surprise, is that as

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:22.879
population increased, all these

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:25.519
resources became even more abundant.

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:27.529
So he and Ehrlich

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:29.449
began to have this quite

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:31.309
public dispute about what was

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:32.599
going to happen in the future, what

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:33.919
is that relationship between

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:35.569
population resources?

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:37.399
And it finally ended up in a

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:38.209
bet.

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:40.069
And Julian said,

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:42.319
Look, pick any nonrenewable

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:44.299
resource for any period over a year

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:45.589
and I'll bet you that it's going to

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:46.609
become more abundant.

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:47.929
In other words, the price is going

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:49.639
to go to fall, adjusted for

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:50.629
inflation.

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:52.789
And so early picks

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:54.919
five metals: copper,

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:57.229
chromium, nickel, tin and tungsten.

00:04:57.230 --> 00:04:59.089
And they, they had this bet for a

00:04:59.090 --> 00:05:01.109
ten year period from 1980 to

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:02.110
1990.

